{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroDomainVanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroDomainVanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Order.Interval.Finset.Nat","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_ne_zero_of_no_root_of_unity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_ne_zero_of_no_root_of_unity","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HabiroDomainVanishing.lean#L100-L113","doc":"**The converse, and the substrate side.** If no exponent `1 ≤ j ≤ k` makes\n`q^j = 1`, then `(q;q)_k ≠ 0` over a ring without zero divisors — so the sum\nnever truncates and the finite-evaluation argument above is unavailable.\n\nThe substrate anchor is exactly this case: a real `q₀ > 1` has `q₀^j > 1` for\nevery `j ≥ 1`, so the hypothesis holds at every `k`. That archimedean fact is the\ncaller's to supply — it is not assumed here, keeping this file free of ordering. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_eventually_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_eventually_zero","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HabiroDomainVanishing.lean#L94-L98","doc":"The same fact in the \"eventually zero\" form the completion argument consumes:\npast `k = n` the q-Pochhammer factor is identically zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_eq_zero_of_pow_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_eq_zero_of_pow_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HabiroDomainVanishing.lean#L81-L92","doc":"**The vanishing mechanism.** If `q` is an `n`-th root of unity with\n`1 ≤ n ≤ k`, then `(q;q)_k = 0` — the `j = n` factor is `1 - q^n = 0`.\n\nThis is why the Habiro ring's elements evaluate at roots of unity: an element is\nan infinite sum `Σ_k f_k(q)·(q;q)_k`, and here all but finitely many terms\nvanish, so the sum terminates and evaluation is a finite computation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf_zero","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HabiroDomainVanishing.lean#L78-L79","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochSelf","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HabiroDomainVanishing.lean#L73-L76","doc":"The q-Pochhammer symbol `(q;q)_k = ∏_{j=1}^{k} (1 - q^j)`, the factor whose\n`k`-adic powers define the Habiro ring's completion. ","signatureChunk":0}]}